  "data": {
    "text": "Forty-eight young adults participated in return for monetary compensation or course credit. Participants were recruited from Wesleyan University, Hartt School of Music, Northeastern University, Berklee College of Music, and the local community in the Greater Boston area. Participants were categorized into Improvisational, Classical, or Minimally Musically Trained (MMT) groups based on their reported musical experience. The Improvisational group (n = 16, 4 Female) was defined by the following criteria: 1) 5+ years of training in music that included improvisation, and 2) Current participation in improvisational musical activities for 1+ hours per week. The Classical group (n = 16, 4 Female) was defined by the following criteria: 1) 5+ years of musical training, and 2) Current participation in non-improvisational musical activities for 1+ hour per week. The minimal musical training (MMT) group (n = 16, 4 Female) included participants who had less than 5 years of previous musical training.\n\nAll participants gave informed consent as approved by the Institutional Review Boards of Wesleyan University, Hartford Hospital, and Northeastern University.\n\nHigh-resolution T1 and resting state images were acquired in either a 3T Siemens Skyra MRI scanner at the Olin Neuropsychiatry Research Center at the Institute of Living or a Siemens Magnetom Prisma 3T scanner at the Northeastern University Biomedical Imaging Center. The anatomical images were acquired using a T1-weighted, 3D, magnetization-prepared, rapid-acquisition, gradient echo (MPRAGE) volume acquisition with a voxel resolution of 0.8 x 0.8 x 0.8 mm3 (TR = 2.4 s, TE = 2.09 ms, flip angle = 8°, FOV = 256 mm). Resting state MRI was acquired as 947 contiguous echo planar imaging (EPI) functional volumes (TR = 475 ms; TE = 30 ms; flip angle = 90, 48 slices; FOV = 240 mm; acquisition voxel size = 3 x 3 x 3 mm3). Participants kept their eyes open and fixated on a cross on the screen during resting state data acquisition. The resting state scan lasted 7.5 min.\n\nResting state and structural MRI preprocessing was carried out using the Statistical Parametric Mapping 12 (SPM12) software with the CONN Toolbox. In order, this consisted of functional realignment and unwarp, functional centering, functional slice time correction, functional outlier detection using the artifact detection tool, functional direct segmentation and normalization to MNI template, structural centering, structural segmentation and normalization to MNI template, and functional smoothing to an 8 mm gaussian kernel. Denoising steps for functional connectivity analysis included correction for confounding effects of white matter and cerebrospinal fluid, and bandpass filtering to 0.008–0.09 Hz.\n\nWe used a functional network atlas that related 14 cortical networks to known cognitive functions (Shirer et al., 2012). Of these 14 networks, 7 aligned with our a priori hypotheses, including three DMN associated regions (vDMN, dDMN, Precuneus network), two ECN associated networks (LECN, RECN), and two salience associated networks (posterior salience network, anterior salience network). Mean time-course of each network was used as a single region-of-interest (ROI), and networks that resulted in clusters demonstrating a significant main effect of group were identified.\n\nCorrelation matrices comparing all 90 regions of the 14-network Stanford atlas were extracted for each participant in each group. These matrices were then exported into MATLAB and analyzed using the Brain Connectivity Toolbox. Each network statistic was computed at a range of correlation thresholds from r = 0.05 to r = 0.5. Individual participants’ measures of degrees, clustering coefficients, strengths, betweenness centrality, and local efficiency were calculated and then averaged across participants for each group, whereas modularity was a single measure for the whole brain that was calculated for each participant.\n\nExample audio files of the prompts are available online at https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.6590489.v1.\n\nImprovising at rest: Differentiating jazz and classical music training with resting state functional connectivity. Alexander Belden; Tima Zeng; Emily Przysinda; Sheeba Arnold Anteraper; Susan Whitfield-Gabrieli; Psyche Loui. NeuroImage 207 (2020) 116384. DOI: 10.1016/j.neuroimage.2019.116384."
  },
